# Source images the wallpapers are derived from. Read by bin/oal-dev-make-wallpapers.
#
# Format, pipe-separated:  id | url | sha256 | gravity | credit | licence | homepage
#
# `gravity` is where the 16:9 crop is taken from (an ImageMagick -gravity value). It is not a
# nicety: iss058e005282 is shot past one of the station's solar arrays, and a centre crop leaves a
# panel grid in the corner of every wallpaper derived from it.
#
# These files are deliberately NOT committed. The repository went from ~90 MB to 37 MB when the 68
# unlicensed wallpapers were purged, and re-adding 20 MB of source photography to generate 20
# derived images would undo a good part of that. They are fetched into $OAL_DEV_CACHE and verified
# against the hash below, exactly the way bin/oal-dev-upstream-fetch treats the upstream tarball and
# test/vm/vm-image treats the Arch cloud image. A pinned hash also means "same source, same
# wallpaper" survives NASA reorganising a URL.
#
# Why public domain specifically: docs/asset-audit-findings.md is the record of what happens
# otherwise. NASA-produced imagery is not subject to copyright protection in the United States
# (https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/), which makes it one of the very few
# large bodies of photography a fork can redistribute and re-tone without asking anyone. Credit is
# requested rather than required; NOTICE gives it anyway, per derived file.
#
# Choosing them is mostly rejection. A wallpaper is empty space with something in one corner, so it
# has to be dark, one frame, and calm enough to sit behind icons. Two candidates were dropped after
# rendering all 20 themes and looking at the sheet: southern_ring_nebula is a two-panel NIRCam/MIRI
# comparison figure and put a seam down the middle of seven themes, and iss040e062704 has the
# station's robotic arm across the top, which reads as a photograph of a spacecraft rather than a
# background.

